prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 5–9 sentences. I'm in the dev loop on stream 2 of an active initiative — the feature branch is `feat/session-store-migration`, currently at a clean Quality+Test state. The user just told me "stream 1 (the auth middleware refactor) just merged to main after CR review." What do I do next, in order, and why is plain `git push --force` wrong here?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers the cross-stream rebase mechanic from SKILL.md §10 in order. (1) **Fetch the new mainline.** `git fetch origin && git rebase origin/main` from stream 2's branch. Resolve conflicts in place. The rationale is sequential-stream design: stream 2's diff is meaningful only against stream 1's shipped state, so staying on the pre-rebase base means reviewing against a stale mainline. (2) **Re-run §7 Quality + Test + Code Review Gates** for the slice that intersects the rebased change. Threshold and retry-budget semantics are unchanged. The agent must NOT push the rebased branch with stale gate evidence — the rebase may have changed which lines run, which tests cover them, and what the reviewer sees. (3) **Force-push with `git push --force-with-lease`** — never plain `--force`. Plain `--force` is unsafe against concurrent pushes (a teammate's commit on the same branch silently disappears); `--force-with-lease` aborts if the remote moved since the agent's last fetch. This is also the *only* place §9's "new commits, never amends" rule yields — the sequential-stream contract requires rebasing. (4) **Update the open PR/CR body** if the rebase substantially changed the diff (e.g. shrunk because stream 1's changes are now in main); reviewers shouldn't be reading against a stale summary. Wrong answers: ignoring stream 1's merge and continuing on the pre-rebase base; using plain `git push --force`; skipping the §7 re-run after rebase; suggesting a merge commit from main into the feature branch instead of a rebase; rebasing but forgetting to update the PR body when the diff shrunk.
tools: claude,kiro
